I'm updating my portfolio site and need a factual export of my professional work.
Scan my local repositories and GitHub organisations, then write two files:
`portfolio-export.json` and `portfolio-export.md` in the current directory.

## Who I am in git history
Treat commits as mine if the author matches any of: my `git config user.email`,
`akinkunmitolulope23@gmail.com`, GitHub user `H4LV3D`, or the names
"Toluwalope", "Tolu" or "Akinkunmi". List any other identities you find that
look like me, so I can confirm them.

## What to scan
1. Every git repo under the current directory, up to 3 levels deep. Include
   monorepos like `events-workspace` and treat each app in `apps/*` or
   `packages/*` as its own project.
2. If the `gh` CLI is logged in: run `gh org list`, then for every org
   (especially Circular Net / CircularNet and LawxTech) run
   `gh repo list <org> --limit 200 --json name,description,visibility,homepageUrl,url,pushedAt,primaryLanguage`
   and include any repo with commits by me. Use `gh api` for each repo's
   description, homepage and topics. Shallow-clone into a temp folder only if a
   repo isn't already on disk.

## For each project, gather
- Product: what it does and who it's for (from README, docs, app.json/app.config,
  page metadata, marketing copy, store listings in the repo).
- My role and period: my first and last commit dates, my commit count vs the
  total, and the areas I touched (top directories by my changed lines).
- What I built: summarise my commits into 4–8 feature bullets, written as
  outcomes, e.g. "Built the ticket scanner and attendee check-in flow".
  Group them by platform (web, mobile, backend, shared).
- Stack: frameworks and key libraries from package.json, app.json, pubspec,
  requirements and so on (e.g. Next.js, Expo/React Native, Tailwind, Prisma).
- Live links: production URLs, App Store and Play Store links, and the
  marketing site. Search README, package.json `homepage`, app.json, metadata or
  SEO files, `.env.example`, and deploy configs (vercel.json, eas.json, etc.).
  Mark each link `confirmed` (clearly production) or `guess`.
- Metrics: only if written down in the repo or docs (users, downloads,
  ratings, performance numbers). Never invent numbers.
- Visuals: paths to existing screenshots, app icons or store images I could
  reuse (just list the paths).
- Visibility: public or private.

## Privacy rules (strict)
- Never copy source code, API keys, tokens, `.env` values, Google Maps or
  Firebase keys, credentials, internal hostnames, customer names or data, or
  Jira ticket numbers into the output. If a description needs a secret to make
  sense, leave it out.
- For private repos: describe the product and my contribution only, and set
  `links.code` to null.
- If you can't tell whether something is confidential, include it with
  `"needsReview": true`.

